(* ============================================================================
   PART 9: SEMANTIC CONSTRAINTS (GUARDS + OPERATIONAL SEMANTICS; the
   rules a context-free production cannot carry, stated formally)
   ============================================================================ *)

(*
   1. WORD BOUNDARIES FOR EMPHASIS -- the templates of CARVE-P3-013, read
      as the CAN OPEN and CAN CLOSE tests of §9.

   2. MATCHING FENCES -- FORMALIZED. Stated as `where` guards on the
      productions (GUARD NOTATION, header):
      - code_fence_close: same fence character, len(close) >= len(open); the
        closer is optional and an unclosed fence runs to the end of its host
        (PART 0 S1, §10 I4);
      - colon_fence_close (§12): len(close) = len(open), matched against the
        INNERMOST open colon fence; the closer is optional and an unclosed
        container ends at its host boundary (CARVE-P9-084);
      - comment_block_close: len(close) = len(open). Only the LEADING RUN of
        `%` is compared -- trailing text on either fence line is insignificant,
        and an opener with no matching closer ahead opens nothing (§28).

   3. NO BARE SAME-TYPE NESTING -- §9 R4 and SAME-DELIMITER ADJACENCY;
      a braced child may repeat its parent's kind (§9 R1).

   4. CAPTION PLACEMENT -- ONE RULE, TWO SPELLINGS (caption_slot in PART 2).
      A `^ ` caption attaches to exactly seven captionable hosts: an image
      paragraph, a blockquote, a table, a fenced code block
      (numbered, like every host, only when the caption has a `#` placeholder,
      PART 9R R5), a standalone
      display-math block (the block must be solely the
      `$$`…`` span), the CLOSING fence of a bare `::: figure` container
      (a composite FIGURE GROUP -- CARVE-P9-001), or the CLOSING fence of a
      `quote_block`. The allowance is the same for all
      seven: adjacent OR exactly one blank line attaches, two blank lines
      detach and leave the `^ ` line an ordinary paragraph.
      A STRUCTURAL-ONLY LINE between host and marker detaches too where it
      changes the open container (PART 2, the caption's `blank_line`), and is
      not one of the blanks that allowance counts -- see CARVE-P9-080. A bare
      `>` that continues the host quote changes nothing: `> q`, `>`, `^ cap`
      captions the quote. An accepted `+` reads the next line inside its
      host, and that line never captions the host itself (carve#4639): `> q`,
      `+`, `^ c` is a quote holding the paragraphs `q` and `^ c`.

      THE SEVENTH IS THE SECOND SPELLING OF THE SECOND. `quote_block` is a
      block quote and captions as a figure. Like `figure_group`, its caption
      attaches at the closing fence; captions after other `:::` kinds are
      ordinary paragraphs.

      WHERE THAT ALLOWANCE IS WRITTEN DOWN differs. `code_block`, `blockquote`,
      `table`, `figure_group` and `quote_block` end in `[caption_slot]`, whose
      optional blank line specifies the allowance. Image-only and standalone
      display-math paragraphs have no separate production; this clause defines
      their caption attachment. The host enumeration is closed: other
      paragraphs are not captionable.
      Trailing attributes on the sole display-math span stay on that span and
      do not prevent the paragraph from taking a following caption.

   4b. WHAT A CAPTION MAKES OF ITS HOST -- EXPLANATORY. The `figure` node is
      the GENERIC captioned wrapper, not the semantic notion "figure". A
      captioned code block is a `figure` whose target is the code block, a
      captioned standalone display-math block is a `figure` whose target is
      that paragraph, and a captioned quote is a `figure` whose target is the
      quote. What a captioned thing is CALLED and counted as comes from the
      caption's own label -- the text before the `#` placeholder, which is the
      counter bucket key (PART 2, CAPTION NUMBER PLACEHOLDER) -- and never from
      the host's node type. `^ Listing #: …` on a code block yields "Listing 1"
      because the author wrote "Listing", not because a code block is a listing.

   4c. A COMPOSITE FIGURE GROUPS ITS PANELS UNDER ONE CAPTION -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-001]
      (governs `figure_group` in PART 2). A bare `::: figure` container holds
      ordered panels. The caption after its closing fence belongs to the group.
      The AST is `figure_group` (PART 12 §16), with `children`, optional
      `caption`, attributes and position, and no `target`, title or label.
      Its type distinguishes it from the existing `figure` wrapper.

      THE OPENER IS BARE OR IT IS NOT THIS PRODUCTION. `figure_group_open`
      contains only the fence, separator and `figure`. It always produces a
      group, including with zero or one panel. A quoted title or label instead
      matches `admonition_open`, preserving a generic Tier-2
      `<div class="figure">` and its metadata; lint reports
      `figure-group-opener-metadata`. The group's metadata channel is its caption.

      PANELS ARE THE DIRECT CHILDREN THE INNER RULES ALREADY MAKE CAPTIONABLE
      THINGS OF. Parse the body under §4. Direct `figure` children (captioned
      images, code, math, quotes or promoted reference images) and `table`
      children, captioned or not, are panels in source order. Preserve all
      other content in place, including paragraphs, lists, uncaptioned quotes
      and nested containers; a strict profile lints it. Renderers MUST NOT
      drop or reattach it. An uncaptioned quote may therefore share the group's
      caption without becoming a panel. A table panel keeps its `<caption>` and its attributes on the `table`
      element; the panel wrapper adds no caption.

      THE GROUP CAPTION ATTACHES AT THE CLOSER, THIS KIND ONLY. A caption,
      including its normal multiline form, attaches adjacent to or one blank
      line below the closing fence. Two blanks detach it into an ordinary
      paragraph. Other `:::` kinds keep their caption behavior. Captions inside
      the fence attach only to their local hosts.

      THE GROUP IS ONE NUMBERING UNIT (PART 9R R5). Its caption's `#` draws one
      number from the label's sequence. Panels draw no document-sequence number:
      a panel caption's `#` stays literal and lints as `figure-group-panel-number`.
      Only the group gets a list-of-figures entry. Panel ids resolve crossrefs
      (R4) with the group's number and a letter in panel order, a..z then aa,
      ab, ..., such as "Figure 2a"; the group's id resolves as "Figure 2".
      Letters occur only in crossref text; no visible panel letter is generated.
      Panel ids register as caption targets only when the group draws a number.
      In an unnumbered group they remain anchors, like ids on uncaptioned figures.

      THE GROUP NUMBERS WHERE IT BEGINS, NOT WHERE ITS CAPTION SITS. Number
      any captionable host at its own position, not at its caption line.
      A figure group begins at its opening fence and numbers in pre-order,
      before anything inside it.
      A numbered figure in non-panel group content therefore draws after the
      group even when its caption precedes the group's caption in the source.

      GROUPS DO NOT NEST. At any depth inside a group's body, a bare
      `::: figure` opens a generic Tier-2 `<div class="figure">` and lints as
      `figure-group-nested` (318-composite-figures-9), preserving the content.

      LINT, NOT PARSE. All five findings diagnose valid parses:
      `figure-group-opener-metadata`, `figure-group-nested` and
      `figure-group-panel-number` are warnings; `figure-group-empty` and
      `figure-group-single-panel` are strict-profile findings.

      HTML SHAPE (PART 10; corpus 318-composite-figures pins the bytes):

        <figure class="carve-figure-group [hints]" id="...">
          <figure class="carve-figure-panel" id="...">
            ...panel host...
            <figcaption>...</figcaption>
          </figure>
          ...
          <figcaption>Label N: group caption</figcaption>
        </figure>

      PANELS NEST DIRECTLY, WITH NO WRAPPER ELEMENT BETWEEN. Emit no
      intermediate `div`. `carve-figure-group` and `carve-figure-panel` lead
      their respective class lists, followed by authored classes (including
      profile-sanitized layout hints), then id and remaining attributes in
      source order. A panel uses its host's existing `<figure>` shape.
      Omit the trailing `<figcaption>` when the group has no caption. With zero
      panels, preserve content directly in the group. Select panels by their
      `carve-figure-panel` class, never by child position.

   4d. A STRUCTURAL-ONLY LINE RESETS CAPTION SCOPE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-080]
      (constrains `caption_slot` in PART 2). A STRUCTURAL-ONLY LINE is a
      non-blank line that is empty once its block structural prefix is
      removed: a bare `>`, an accepted continuation marker, a bare container
      fence. A list marker with no content and a refused `+` are text. It carries layout and no content. It is NOT a
      blank line outside that quote. Inside a quote, a bare `>` is that quote's
      blank line and counts toward a contained host's one-blank caption allowance.

      WHERE IT CHANGES THE OPEN CONTAINER, IT ENDS ANY PENDING CAPTION SCOPE.
      A structural-only line that opens or closes a container ends the scope
      of a caption-eligible host above it, the way a blank line beyond the
      one-blank allowance does.

      AND THE CONTAINER IT OPENS TAKES NO CAPTION OF ITS OWN WHILE IT HAS NO
      CONTENT. A host whose extent is delimited by its OWN structural prefix
      -- a `>` blockquote -- and whose every line is structural-only holds
      nothing a caption could be about, so it is not a captionable host.
      Comment, definition and attribute lines hold no content for this test
      either (CARVE-P0-011): `> %%` / `^ cap` captions nothing, as a bare
      `>` does, while `> a %% c` still captions. An abbreviation definition
      is a definition at document level only (PART 5); in a quote it is
      paragraph text, so `> *[A]: b` / `^ cap` captions. This
      says nothing about hosts delimited by an explicit closing fence, whose
      caption attaches at the closer: a zero-panel `::: figure` still carries
      its group caption (CARVE-P9-001).

      A STRUCTURAL-ONLY LINE INSIDE AN ALREADY-OPEN CONTAINER CHANGES NOTHING.
      `> q` followed by a bare `>` is one quote with content, and it still
      captions. The reset is about the container boundary, not the bare
      marker.

      THE SHAPE THIS PINS (corpus 554). In a table, a blank, a bare `>`, a
      blank, then `^ cap`: the `>` line opens a quote
      that the table's caption scope cannot survive, and that quote has no
      content of its own, so the `^ ` line attaches to nothing and is an
      ordinary paragraph (PART 2, `caption_slot`). Neither the table nor the
      empty quote is captioned.

      WHAT THIS CLAUSE DOES NOT REACH. Only lines that are structural-only. A
      further `^ ` line after a captioned host, and a `^ ` line with no
      captionable host above it, are governed by §4 and by PART 2's caption
      continuation rules, and are unchanged here.

   5. TABLE MODEL -- OPERATIONAL SEMANTICS (cell split, row validity,
      header cells, span walk, continuation rows, delimiter row, row
      attributes). STATE: a parsed table is a grid of logical cells;
      consumed(r,c) marks a position absorbed by a span originating at
      another cell; avail(r,c) := a cell was emitted at (r,c) and NOT
      consumed(r,c).
      DELIMITED BODY. A preceding boolean `{table}` attribute
         makes a fence whose language is exactly `csv` or `tsv` an ordinary
         `table` node rather than a `code_block`. The flag is consumed; other
         table attributes, including `header-rows`, `footer-rows`, `aligns`,
         `valigns` and `widths`, apply to that table. An unmarked fence stays
         code. On any other block, or where no table results, `table` is an
         ordinary attribute (`<pre table="">`, `<p table="">`). A title or label on the fence has no table slot and is
         discarded. A caption after the fence becomes the table's caption and is
         numbered by its own label. An empty-valued `table=""` is
         equivalent to the boolean flag under the attribute rules.

         `csv` reads comma-separated records with RFC 4180 double-quote
         escaping, including a quoted field containing a newline. `tsv`
         substitutes TAB for comma and uses the same quoted-field rule.
         Record separators are LF after Carve's source ingestion; the final
         separator is optional. One final separator terminates the final record without adding
         another. A preceding separator still creates a blank record. An empty field is an empty text cell; a blank record is one
         empty cell. Fields are LITERAL text: no Carve inline parsing, smart
         typography, span markers, or per-cell attributes are recognized.
         The first row is a header unless `header-rows=0` is supplied; an
         explicit positive `header-rows` marks that many leading rows. A value
         that is no count is absent for this rule, so the first row stays a
         header. A count past the last row marks every row a header cell and
         synthesizes no partition (CARVE-P12-034), so they render in one body group
         at row scope. The
         implicit header uses zero rows when the table is empty or the footer
         consumes every row; an explicit overlapping header and footer is
         invalid under the ordinary table partition rule, so no row is a header
         cell, as on a pipe table (carve#4640).
         Records shorter than the widest record gain empty cells. A record
         wider than the first record is retained, never truncated, and reports
         `delimited-table-wide-row`. An empty fence has zero rows. A malformed
         quoted field, a quote inside an unquoted field, or text after a
         closing quote leaves the fence as code, with its ordinary attributes
         (including `table`), and reports `delimited-table-malformed`. The table AST
         records `sourceFormat` as `csv` or `tsv`, so the canonical Carve
         writer keeps the delimited form. It may normalize quotes and record
         separators. External files, spans and per-cell attributes have no
         delimited-body spelling.
      T1 CELL SPLIT (escape scan, cell-splitting-aware). Scanning a row
         left to right OUTSIDE code spans: an unescaped `|` separates
         cells; `\|` and a `|` inside a code span are content. (The
         code-span state carried across the scan is what makes this
         non-context-free.)
      T2 VALID TABLE ROW. ONE predicate, used both for the §10 I1
         interruption test and for opening a table at a block start:
         valid_row(L) := L's first non-indent character is `|` AND its last
         non-whitespace character is `|` AND at least one cell lies between.
         (T8's row attribute block is glued to that closing `|`, so a row
         ending `|{.x}` still qualifies -- strip the block, then test.)
         A line-initial `|` WITHOUT the closing `|` is prose, wherever it
         appears: `| a | b` neither interrupts a paragraph nor opens a table
         (corpus 140). This matches the `standard_row` production, which
         ends in `'|'`; there is no lenient open form.
         The two uses may never disagree: a line the block parser builds a
         table from but the §17 sub-block test calls prose would make a list
         item loose AND fill it with a table.
         Lint reports `table-shaped-paragraph` at the first line of a
         table-shaped run lacking its closing pipe. A non-table-shaped
         paragraph does not qualify. A pipe-table body row with more or
         fewer effective cells than its header renders as written and lint
         reports `table-row-cell-count` at that row. Colspan and rowspan
         markers count the cells they span; a correctly spanned row is quiet.
         A continuation row that appends a cell (T6) gets the same finding on
         its `+` line, with or without a header.

         A CELL SLOT IS COUNTED, NOT WEIGHED -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-082]. The
         delimiters are T1's: an escaped `|` and a `|` inside a code span
         are content. A slot lies between two consecutive delimiters, and
         EMPTY CONTENT IS VALID CONTENT for it, so a row whose every cell is
         blank is a row:

             | |    ||        one data cell, empty children
             | | |  |||       two data cells
             ||||             three data cells
             |                no closing delimiter, no slot: prose

         No comment, literal or whitespace node is synthesized for a blank
         cell. Only ASCII space padding is removed (PART 7); a tab between
         the pipes is content, so `|<TAB>|` is a cell holding a tab. The rule
         is the same for data and header cells, at a block start, after an
         open paragraph and inside a table. An empty MARKED cell still needs
         T11's space: `|= |` is an empty header cell and `|=|` a data cell
         holding `=`; `|> |` is an empty right-aligned cell and `|>|` holds
         `>`; `|{#x} |` is an empty cell with id `x` and `|{#x}|` carries no
         cell attributes. This gives no spelling to a table with zero rows,
         and it leaves T6, T7 and T8 as they are: a continuation row still
         creates no row, and a blank first row above a delimiter row is
         promoted to an empty header row like any other first row.
      T3 HEADER CELL. A leading `=` GLUED to the cell's opening `|` marks a
         HEADER cell; `\=` yields a data cell whose text starts with `=`.
      T4 SPAN MARKERS. A cell whose WHOLE content is `^` (rowspan) or `<`
         (colspan) is a span marker; ANY other content disqualifies it --
         `| {.x} < |` is an ordinary cell with literal text `{.x} <`, while
         `|{.x} < |` is an attributed cell holding a literal `<` (T10).
         Neither is a span. Leading or trailing U+0020 padding
         does not disqualify the marker. `|<|`, `| <|`, `|< |` and `| < |`
         are spans, as are all four corresponding `^` forms. A lone span
         glyph takes precedence over an alignment run on an empty cell.
         Any consumed marker run disqualifies a span. Thus `|= <|` and
         `|= ^|` are header cells holding literal `<` and `^`, while
         `|?^ <|` is a vertically aligned cell holding literal `<`.
      T5 SPAN WALK -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-002], a total function. Markers
         resolve in row-major order. Every position belongs to the cell whose
         rectangle covers it; a span never covers a position another cell
         owns, so the emitted grid has no overlap.
           `<` at (r,c): the cell covering (r,c-1) gains one column, if that
             cell starts in row r. A cell that started above would widen
             across rows already placed.
           `^` at (r,c): the cell covering (r-1,c) gains one row, if its
             left edge is column c and every other position of the new row
             strip it would cover, (r,c+1) to (r,c+colspan-1), is a span
             marker not yet resolved or has no cell. Those markers resolve
             into the same cell and render nothing.
         The marker's position is then consumed. A `^` MAY reach a HEADER
         cell: the rowspan crosses the authored header/body partition
         (`<th rowspan="N">`), for native `|=` and GFM-promoted headers
         alike (corpus 101-table-header-cell-rowspan). PART 10 §7 puts every
         row in one `<tbody>` when a rowspan crosses a row-group boundary.
         NO origin -> the marker renders as an EMPTY cell (`<td></td>` /
         `<th></th>`, no content except the continuation fragments T6 gives
         it, no span); never dropped, never literal. It is a cell like any
         other, so a later `<` in its row may widen it: `| ^ | < |` is one
         `<td colspan="2">`.
         Covers uniformly:
           * ORPHAN -- `^` in the FIRST row / `<` in the FIRST column
             (corpus 98-table-span-marker-in-first-column);
           * BLOCKED -- the neighbor is covered by a cell that started in an
             earlier row (`<`), or the new row strip would cover a cell
             (`^`) (corpus 106-blocked-span-marker-renders-as-empty-cell,
             107-colspan-marker-scans-left-past-a-consumed-cell).
         A 2x2 cell is `| a | < |` over `| ^ | < |` (or `| ^ | ^ |`).
      T6 CONTINUATION ROWS (`+` as the first non-indent character instead of `|`).
         Appends per-column onto the row ABOVE: each non-empty cell joins
         its column's cell content with a single space (soft wrap); empty
         cells append nothing; no `<tr>` is produced. Under rowspan the
         joined content belongs to the SPANNING cell, whether the continuation
         stands before or after the `^` row (corpus
         64-table-rowspan-with-multi-line-content). A `+` fragment on a span
         marker joins the cell the T5 walk resolves the marker into, in
         row-major order. A marker with no origin, ORPHAN or BLOCKED (T5), is
         a cell of its own: it renders no marker, and the fragments on it are
         its content (carve#3151). A table cannot BEGIN with a continuation row (PART 2 `table`). The AST
         keeps each join as a `cell_wrap` marker, so a writer can put the
         `+` lines back (CARVE-P12-069).

         A CELL PAST THE ROW'S LAST COLUMN IS A NEW CELL OF THE ROW ABOVE. A
         non-empty fragment in a slot where that row has no cell appends one,
         so no text is lost. A column it skips gets an empty cell, and an empty
         slot past the last cell appends nothing. The fragment is the cell's
         content and never a T5 span marker, so `^` and `<` there are text. The
         new cell is a header cell when every other cell of the row is. A `^`
         in that row counts as a data cell for this test; only a `<` counts as
         the cell it extends (carve#4640). A later
         `+` line joins onto it like any cell. Lint reports the row at the
         `+` line (T2).

         THE ROW ABOVE IS A LINE, NOT A `<tr>` -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-069]. What
         a continuation joins is the row above it in the SOURCE. T7 CONSUMES
         the delimiter row - it produces no `<tr>` and is not a row of the
         table - so a `+` line directly under one has no row above it to join
         and is ordinary prose, and corpus 115 pins it. An invisible line (a
         `%%` comment, an attribute line) ends the table too, so a `+` line
         after it is prose as well: `| a |`, `%% c`, `+ b |` is a table and a
         paragraph `+ b |`.

         AND THE ROW ABOVE IS A LINE OF THE SAME CONTAINER. A table in a
         quote does not precede a continuation line lacking that quote's prefix.
         In the following example the item's preceding block is a quote:

             - > | a |
               + b |

         T6 declines and the prose reopens the item's paragraph. The control
         `- | a |` followed by `  | b |` has two standard rows in the item's
         own table; the second joins without opening a paragraph.

         HEADER-NESS IS NOT THE DISCRIMINATOR. Native all-header rows, including
         later rows, accept continuations. A delimiter row directly above the
         continuation makes it decline, including under a native header row.

         AN ALL-EMPTY CONTINUATION ROW IS A CONTINUATION ROW. It opens no
         table and produces no `<tr>`; T2 counts its slots the way it counts
         a standard row's, and T6 decides what they do. Its empty cells append
         nothing. `+ | |` below `| a | b |` and `+ |`
         below `| a |` are both absorbed.

         A CONTINUATION ROW LEAVES NO PARAGRAPH OPEN -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-003].
         A container's last block remains a table after a continuation row.
         Under PART 0 S4, a following flush-left line ends the container and becomes
         a document paragraph, as after a standard row. This applies to lists,
         quotes and definition bodies.

         THE OPPOSITE DIRECTION IS UNCHANGED. With no table above it, `+ ...|`
         is prose, opens a paragraph and a following flush-left line folds lazily
         (corpus 326-...-26 and -28).

         A QUOTE IS ASKED ITS OWN BODY. Apply PART 0 S4 to the quote's entire body,
         rather than peeling one physical line. Its last block determines
         whether a paragraph remains open.

         A QUOTE INSIDE A QUOTE IS ASKED THE SAME QUESTION. Follow the last
         child quote recursively to its own last block (PART 0 S4).
      T7 GFM DELIMITER ROW -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-004]. delimiter_row(R) := R is EXACTLY
         the SECOND row of the table AND every cell matches delimiter_cell
         (optional ws, optional `:`, one or more `-`, optional `:`,
         optional ws -- PART 2; an empty cell `|---||` or other content
         `|-a-|`, `|:|` disqualifies the whole row, which is then an
         ordinary standard_row). Effects:
           1. promote the FIRST row's cells to header (`<thead>`/`<th>`);
           2. set per-column alignment from the colons (`:--` left, `--:`
              right, `:-:` center, bare `---` none), applied to the WHOLE
              column (header + body cells);
           3. CONSUME the row (no `<tr>`), and with it any row attributes
              glued to it, which have no `<tr>` to set (T8).
         Column-count mismatch: unmatched columns promote with default
         alignment. POSITION IS STRICT: a delimiter-shaped row anywhere
         else (first line, or after the second) is an ordinary row whose
         `-` runs are inline content (`---` may smart-render as an em
         dash). Native `|=` header cells and `<`/`>`/`~` alignment markers
         may COEXIST with a delimiter row. Where one column carries both, THE
         MARKER WINS OVER THE DELIMITER COLON (CARVE-P12-039): a marker on a
         head-row cell sets its own physical column, header and body cells, and a
         marker on a body cell sets that cell alone. So `|=> a |` over
         `|:-- |` right-aligns column a in the header and the body. WITH
         SEVERAL HEAD ROWS, THE LAST EXPLICIT MARKER IN A COLUMN SETS THE
         COLUMN, and an omitted marker does not reset an earlier one, the
         order CARVE-P11-031 reads for the Markdown delimiter; each head cell
         keeps its own marker. So `|=> a |` over `|=< b |` over `| 1 |`
         left-aligns the body cell. Corpus 09-tables-3 pins effect 2.
         Among native `|=` markers, only the head-row run (T9) sets column
         defaults. A later `|=` cell after a body row is a row header whose
         marker stays local, even when that later row has only header cells.
         A colspan marker has no alignment run: a header cell spanning several
         columns sets a default only for its ORIGIN column. A delimiter colon
         in a covered column remains that column's default; the origin's marker
         does not overwrite it. Corpus 659 pins this covered-column rule.
      T8 ROW ATTRIBUTES -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-005]. A `{…}` GLUED to a row's closing `|`
         (no intervening space) sets that row's `<tr>` attributes -- the
         row-level twin of a cell's attribute block, same syntax, same
         validity rule, and UNCHANGED by T10: a row's block still follows
         the row's last `|`, where a cell's follows the cell's marker run.
         The WHOLE payload must be a valid,
         non-empty attribute block (§15); a space before the brace
         (`| a | {.x}`), an empty `{}`, or an invalid payload is NOT a row
         attribute -- the `{` is ordinary content, so the line (ending in
         `}`, not `|`) is not even a table row. Applies to header and body
         rows; composes with the delimiter row and with cell attributes:
         `|{.c} a |{.r}` -> `<tr class="r">` with `<td class="c">`. Pinned
         by corpus 100-table-row-attributes. The row block follows the last
         `|`, so a pipe in one of its quoted values is no delimiter:
         `| a |{k="b|c"}` gives `<tr k="b|c">`. A cell's block sits inside
         the cell, where a raw pipe still ends the cell, so `|{k="a|b"} x |`
         is two cells and `\|` spells the pipe there (PART 2, table rows).

      T9 A HEADER CELL STATES WHAT IT HEADS -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-006]. Every `th` carries a
         `scope`: `col` for a cell in the head-row run, `row` for one below it.
         `|=` already distinguishes the two positions -- a header cell in the
         first rows opens `<thead>`, one in a later row is a row header inside
         `<tbody>` (corpus 09-tables-7) -- so the attribute states an
         association the table already has rather than adding a concept.
         The head-row run is the leading rows that own a cell and in which
         every cell a row owns is a header cell; a span placeholder belongs
         to the cell covering it, so `|= A | < |` heads its table
         (CARVE-P11-056).

         An authored `scope` replaces the default (PART 10 §1), so
         `|={scope="colgroup"} a |` -- a header cell carrying attributes,
         which follows T10 -- is
         `<th scope="colgroup">` and not
         `<th scope="col" scope="colgroup">`. That is how `colgroup` and
         `rowgroup`, which have no marker spelling here, stay reachable: two
         attributes of one name is not valid HTML and is not an override.

         `{Scope="colgroup"}` remains authored as `Scope` and suppresses the
         generated default under the same PART 10 §1 rule.

         Otherwise `scope` precedes `rowspan`, `colspan` and the authored
         block in the serialization.

         An authored `colspan` or `rowspan` on a cell is always dropped,
         whether or not a marker computes a span on that axis. Only the
         span markers shape the grid: `|{colspan=2} a | b |` writes
         `<td>a</td><td>b</td>`.

      T10 CELL ATTRIBUTES BIND LAST -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-007]. A cell's `{…}` block
         attaches AFTER the kind marker and AFTER the alignment marker, in
         every cell. One order, both productions (PART 2 `header_cell`,
         `data_cell`):

             |={.x} h |      header, attributed
             |=~{.x} h |     header, centered, attributed
             |>{.x} d |      data, right-aligned, attributed
             |{.x} d |       data, no marker, attributed

         The block is GLUED: to the marker run where the cell has one, to
         the opening `|` where it has none. A space anywhere in front of it
         makes it ordinary content (T4's `| {.x} < |` is unchanged), and the
         validity rule is T8's.

         The canonical writer uses this order. The `|{#x}< content |` spelling
         is parsed as unaligned literal content; row attributes (T8) are
         unaffected.

      T11 A MARKER RUN ENDS AT A SPACE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-008]. A cell's marker run is
         the kind marker `=`, the alignment run and the attribute block, in
         T10's order. A cell carrying ANY of the three must follow the run
         with one literal space. Without it there is no run at all, and every
         character of it is ordinary cell content:

             |= a |          header cell, content `a`
             |=a |           data cell, content `=a`
             |=hot= |        data cell; its inline content `=hot=` is a highlight
             |{#x} =R |      data cell with `id="x"`, content `=R`
             |{#x}=R|        data cell, content `{#x}=R`

         A cell with NO run is unchanged -- `| a |` and `|a|` hold `a`, and
         `||` is one empty cell (CARVE-P9-082).

         THE CLOSING PIPE IS NOT A TERMINATOR. `|=|` and `|{#x}|` carry no
         run; an empty marked cell is `|= |` and `|{#x} |` -- which is what
         CARVE-P11-014's canonical writer already emits, and what `|> |` already
         required of the alignment run. NEITHER IS A TAB: PART 7 gives a tab
         no padding role inline, so `|=<TAB>h |` is content too.

         AN ESCAPE REACHES THE OTHER READING where a space cannot. `|\= a |`
         is a data cell whose content is a literal `= a`, unchanged here.

         `|=a |` and `|{#x}=R|` are data-cell content. CARVE-P11-014's writer emits a
         space after every prefix.

         TABLE MARKER-PADDING DIAGNOSTICS SHARE ONE ID -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-009]. The id is
         `table-marker-run-padding`. One id
         for the whole run, because T11 is one rule for the whole run: it
         reports a glued kind marker, a glued alignment run and a glued
         attribute block alike, and `fmt --migrate` repairs any of them by
         inserting the space.

   6. REFERENCE RESOLUTION -- PART 9R R1-R3.

   7. MENTION/TAG BOUNDARIES
      - @ and # only start mentions/tags at word boundaries
      - email@domain.com is NOT a mention
      - The opening `_` of a formed span is start of content and a closing
        `_` is a guard character: `_@a_` holds a mention, `_a_#b` has no tag
      - The name runs over letters, digits, `_`, `-`, and INTERIOR dots
        (a dot followed by another name character: `@john.doe`,
        `#release-1.0`); a trailing dot is sentence punctuation, not part
        of the name

   8. SMART TYPOGRAPHY CONTEXT
      - Only applies outside code spans/blocks
      - Patterns must match exactly (not partial)
      - FRACTIONS ARE NOT CONVERTED. `1/2`, `3/4`, etc. stay literal --
        they collide with dates (`1/2/2024`) and paths, and djot has no
        fractions (recorded in dismissed-syntax.md). Converted set:
        dashes, ellipsis, smart quotes, arrows, comparisons, `+-`, and
        the symbol `(tm)`. `(c)` and `(r)` are not converted (carve#4808).
      - SMART TYPOGRAPHY RUNS BY DEFAULT -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-010]. The substitution belongs to
        the default inline layer, not to an opt-in feature: a conformant
        implementation performs it with NO extension registered. A locale
        or glyph extension (a German / Swiss quote set, say) selects WHICH
        characters are emitted; it does NOT decide WHETHER the substitution
        runs, and removing such an extension does not disable the
        transform.
      - HOSTS MAY OFFER A DOCUMENT-WIDE SMART TYPOGRAPHY SWITCH -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-011]. A host MAY expose
        one document-global switch, `smartTypography`, defaulting to TRUE
        where offered. A host that omits the switch stays conformant. ACCEPTING IT AND
        IGNORING IT IS NOT -- a host that takes `smartTypography: false`
        without error and emits the glyphs anyway has told the caller the
        document is configured when it is not, which is worse than the
        rejection an unimplemented option should produce. Omitting and
        implementing are both fine; the silent middle is the one state this
        clause forbids.

        Where it IS offered and set to FALSE:
        * PARSING IS UNCHANGED. The nodes below are still produced, so the
          AST does not depend on the switch. What changes is rendering: a
          presentation renderer emits the node's SOURCE RUN instead of its
          glyph, exactly as the canonical writer already does. Every trigger
          character therefore survives as the ASCII the author typed --
          dashes, ellipsis, quotes, arrows, comparisons, and the typographic
          symbols `(tm)` `+-`;
        * the `:name:` symbol production is UNAFFECTED; it is not a
          `smart_typography` production, and its precedence over the
          typographic forms is unchanged;
        * escape handling is UNAFFECTED: `\"`, `\-`, `\.` still consume the
          backslash and yield the bare character (PART 7). Turning the
          switch off changes which UNESCAPED characters convert, nothing
          about escapes;
        * heading ids are BYTE-IDENTICAL either way. Ids are computed from
          the ASCII source (the reverse pass in HEADING IDENTIFIERS above),
          so with the switch off that pass has nothing to reverse -- it is
          a no-op, not a different result;
        * the switch is DOCUMENT-GLOBAL and applies to EVERY target.
          PER-TARGET DEFAULTS ARE NON-CONFORMANT (on for HTML, off for
          Markdown / plain / ANSI): one source must carry the same text on
          every target, and a target-dependent default would let
          `to_html(x)` and `to_markdown(x)` disagree about what the
          document says.
        A host MAY additionally accept a per-family value on the same switch.
        The only ruled family is `quotes`: `smartTypography: { quotes: false }`
        emits the SOURCE RUN for `left_double_quote`, `right_double_quote`,
        `left_single_quote`, and `right_single_quote`, including mid-word
        apostrophes. Every other smart-punctuation kind still emits its glyph.
        An omitted `quotes` field or `quotes: true` retains the default glyph
        output. The boolean FALSE and source mode still disable ALL families;
        existing boolean and mode values keep their meaning. API spelling is
        host-specific; PHP and Rust may expose an equivalent quotes-source mode.
        Parsing, AST nodes, escapes, literal curly quotes, and heading ids are
        unchanged. This setting is document-global on HTML, Markdown, plain
        text, and ANSI, including text derived from nodes for labels and alt
        text. A host that does not implement it MUST reject the value rather
        than accept it silently. No in-document switch or other family is ruled.
        Pinned in tests/corpus-optional (features `smart-typography-off` and
        `smart-typography-quotes-off`).
      - SMART TYPOGRAPHY RECORDS SOURCE AND RESOLVED KIND -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-012]. A recognized substitution is its
        own inline node, `smart_punctuation`, carrying BOTH halves: the
        resolved KIND and the author's SOURCE RUN. A presentation renderer
        (HTML, Markdown, plain text, ANSI) emits the glyph for the kind; the
        canonical Carve writer emits the source run, so `fmt` reproduces the
        document instead of normalizing its punctuation. Writing the glyph
        straight into the text buffer is NOT conformant: it discards the
        spelling, after which `...` cannot be told from a literal U+2026 and
        the writer has nothing to reproduce.
        * SOURCE OUTPUT ON THE MARKDOWN TARGET -- OPTIONAL, NORMATIVE WHERE
          OFFERED. The glyph above is the DEFAULT on every presentation
          target and stays so. An implementation MAY additionally offer, as
          the named optional feature `markdown-typography-source`, a
          Markdown-renderer setting that emits the SOURCE RUN instead. An
          implementation that omits it stays conformant. Where it IS
          offered:
          - IT IS NOT A DEFAULT AND NOT DOCUMENT-GLOBAL. It is asked for per
            render call, on the Markdown target only. The `smartTypography`
            switch above is untouched, and so is its rule that per-target
            DEFAULTS are non-conformant: nothing here changes what any
            target emits unless a caller asks;
          - PARSING IS UNCHANGED, exactly as for the off switch. The same
            nodes are produced and the AST does not depend on the setting;
          - THE OTHER PRESENTATION TARGETS MUST NOT OFFER IT. Markdown is
            re-parseable source, which is what makes the canonical writer's
            argument apply to it: the source run survives a re-parse as the
            same node, while the glyph cannot be told from a literal one.
            HTML, plain text and ANSI are terminal presentations, where the
            glyph is the only sensible output and a source run would just be
            worse typography;
          - IT REVERSES ONLY WHAT THE RENDERER SUBSTITUTED. A quote the
            author typed as a typographic character is not a
            `smart_punctuation` node and is emitted unchanged either way.
          Pinned in tests/corpus-optional (feature
          `markdown-typography-source`, target `markdown`).
        * KIND NAMES ARE SPEC SURFACE -- an implementation inventing its own
          spelling breaks every AST consumer. The sixteen table-resolved
          kinds are `ellipsis`, `em_dash`, `en_dash`, `left_right_arrow`,
          `rightwards_arrow`, `leftwards_arrow`, `rightwards_double_arrow`,
          `leftwards_double_arrow`, `left_right_double_arrow`,
          `less_than_or_equal`, `greater_than_or_equal`, `not_equal`,
          `plus_minus`, `copyright`, `registered`, `trademark`. The four
          quote kinds are `left_double_quote`, `right_double_quote`,
          `left_single_quote`, `right_single_quote`.
        * A QUOTE NODE ALSO CARRIES ITS RESOLVED GLYPH. Quote characters are
          locale-dependent (a German or Swiss set), and the choice is made
          during parsing, so the node records the character rather than
          leaving it to the kind table. Every other kind resolves through the
          table above, which is why the quote kinds are absent from it.
        * A DASH RUN PARTITIONS INTO ONE NODE PER RESOLVED GLYPH, each
          carrying the hyphens it came from, so `----` (two en dashes)
          round-trips to exactly four hyphens.
        * FOR PROFILES the node folds into the `text` trust class. It is
          ordinary visible prose, not a distinct capability, so it is not
          separately nameable in a profile (profiles.md).
        See divergence-from-djot.md section 12 for how this compares to
        Djot's container-node model.

   AUTHORING LINT. These findings report source that resembles a construct but
   renders differently, without changing the parse or HTML. Unless stated
   otherwise they are on by default. The finding points at the source line
   carrying the failed spelling, and an escaped or verbatim example is quiet.
   Source-pattern findings inspect only parsed source that can reach rendered
   content. A recognized line comment, fenced block comment, or inline comment
   excludes its entire source span, including delimiters, from these findings
   at every container depth. Source safety checks that explicitly inspect
   hidden bytes, such as `bidi-control-in-source`, still inspect comment text.

   `table-separator-near-miss` reports a separator-shaped row that does not
   become a delimiter, including a typo in its dash cells or a dash-only cell
   rendered as body content. A valid delimiter is quiet.
   `list-marker-without-blank-line` reports a marker line folded into an open
   preceding paragraph for lack of a blank separator. A separated list and a
   marker inside a list's own body are quiet.
   `unclosed-inline-emphasis` reports a strong or emphasis opener with no
   closer in its paragraph. A paired delimiter is quiet.
   `link-gap-before-destination` reports `] (` where a link destination would
   follow a label. `](` is quiet.
   `markdown-italic-underline` hints that `_word_` renders underline, while
   `/word/` renders italic. It is on by default and can be disabled per
   document. Escaped underscores and code spans are quiet.
   `heading-level-skip` hints when a heading jumps over one or more levels.
   A next-level heading is quiet. `a11y/heading-jump` is a deprecated input
   alias for this rule: enabling or suppressing it selects `heading-level-skip`,
   and every finding reports `heading-level-skip`. Remove the alias at the next
   minor release; two findings for one jump are never emitted.
   PROPOSED CONTINUATION LINT AID (NON-NORMATIVE). A visually lone
   off-column `+` near an open marker host may report its tab-expanded column
   and the host marker column. Offer explicit alignment of the marker and its
   follower, or escaping the `+` as text. Alignment must preview a
   whole-document parse and HTML change and require an intentional action.
   For a recognized refusal, `continuation-marker-refused` should name the
   first failed gate: blank, end, sibling or follower column, with the eligible
   column. Fence payload and harmless literals without a plausible host are
   quiet.
   `continuation-marker-refused` reports every refusal PART 9 §17 names, at
   every host and depth, including the first-block form in a stacked quote
   (`- > +`, `> - +`) and in a description body (`: > +`, `: - +`). A `+`
   that PART 0 S2 reads as ordinary text is not a refusal and is
   quiet.
   `block-opener-overindented` reports a block opener that PART 0 S3 opens
   past its owner's content column, where the owner is the document, a
   quote, a definition body, a footnote body or a colon body: `  # h` at
   document level, `>   # h` in a quote. The opener still opens there; the
   finding names the owner and how far past its column the opener stands,
   and offers removing the indentation or escaping the opener. Openers are a
   heading, thematic break, table row, block quote, code or raw fence, colon
   fence and definition term. A list marker is not: PART 9 §24 C3 and C4
   place it at any indentation, and its own rules cover a misplaced one.
   Comments, definitions, attribute lines and captions are not openers
   either. A table reports once, on its first row. A list item owner reports
   under `list-item-block-overindented` instead (PART 9 §24 C3), so one line
   never carries both. An opener at its owner's content column, and a line
   that stays paragraph text, are quiet.
   `emphasis-marker-before-attributes` reports a bare `*`, `/`, `_`, `~` or
   `=` directly followed by a valid attribute block, and names the reading
   that applies. When the marker closes no span, the marker and the block
   stay text (`a*{.x} b*`). When it closes a span whose content also holds an
   unescaped marker of the same character, the block attaches to that whole
   span (`*a *b*{.x}` attaches to the strong span from column 1). The finding
   offers the braced span (PART 9 §22) or an escape. A closer whose span holds no
   other same marker, such as `*a*{.x}`, is the canonical spelling and is
   quiet, as are a braced span, an escaped marker and code.

   AUTHORING LINT SUPPRESSION. A recognized Carve line comment whose content
   begins with `carve-lint-disable-line`, optionally followed by rule ids
   suppresses those ids on that physical source line. `carve-lint-disable-next-line`
   suppresses them on the immediately following physical source line, even
   across a container boundary. `carve-lint-disable` suppresses them from the
   directive's line through the rest of the document; `carve-lint-enable`
   ends that document scope from its own line. A comma or ASCII space may
   separate ids; an absent id means all rules. The directive begins after the
   `%%` comment opener, with one ASCII space. It is itself a Carve comment and
   never renders. A directive in code, raw content, an inline comment, or a
   fenced comment body is inert. A later enable does not undo a line or
   next-line suppression. For included files, source-line scopes use the
   included file's own lines and document scopes do not cross file boundaries.
   Suppression applies by the finding's reported source line; for any rule id,
   the last document-scope directive selecting it wins. A caller may also
   supply a per-file ignore list of exact file ids; an ignored
   file emits no lint findings, including findings from its included content
   only when that included file id is itself ignored. An ignore list does not
   change parsing, rendering, or other files' findings.
   `unused-link-reference-definition` reports a link reference definition
   whose winning definition is never used by a rendered reference link or
   image. An unresolved or discarded reference use does not count; duplicate
   definitions are diagnosed separately. A used definition is quiet.
   `empty-link-destination` reports a parsed inline link with an empty
   destination, including whitespace-only `()`. An empty reference definition
   remains under `empty-reference-definition`; literal bracket text and an
   escaped parenthesis are quiet.
   `duplicate-reference-url` is opt-in. It reports the later of two distinct
   link reference definitions whose parsed destination strings are identical
   after Carve escape decoding. Repeated use of one definition is quiet.
   `non-descriptive-link-text` is opt-in. It reports a link whose visible text,
   after ASCII whitespace folding and case folding, is `here`, `click here`,
   or `read more`. A nonempty `aria-label` with different, specific text makes
   the link quiet; image alternatives and literal unresolved references are
   outside this rule.
   `emphasis-as-heading` is opt-in. It reports a paragraph whose only visible
   content is one emphasis or strong span, a likely heading substitute. A
   heading, or a paragraph with surrounding visible prose, is quiet.
   `bare-html-tag` hints that an HTML-like tag in text is escaped. A tag in
   code or raw HTML is quiet.
   `comment-shaped-emphasis` hints that a span reads like a C comment
   (carve#4815): an emphasis span delimited by bare `/` whose content begins
   or ends with a literal `*`, or a strong span delimited by bare `*` whose
   content begins or ends with a literal `/`. `/* foo */` renders
   `<em>* foo *</em>`. It reports the opening delimiter. Code, raw content
   and escaped delimiters are quiet.
   `glob-shaped-strong` hints that a strong span reads like a file glob
   (carve#4815): a strong span delimited by bare `*` whose content begins
   with a literal `.`. `*.js,*.ts` renders `<strong>.js,</strong>.ts`. It
   reports the opening delimiter. Code and an escaped `\*` are quiet.
   `html-comment-renders-as-text` warns that an HTML comment in text,
   `<!--` through the first `-->` in one paragraph, renders as visible text
   (carve#4812). The message names `%%` and `%%%` as the Carve comment
   spellings. It reports the `<!--`. A comment in code or raw content, and
   a `<!--` with no `-->` after it in the paragraph, are quiet.
   `markdown-definition-paragraph` reports `term` followed by `: definition`
   when both remain paragraph text. A `:: term` definition is quiet.
   `github-alert-marker` reports `[!NOTE]`, `[!WARNING]` and the other GitHub
   alert labels in a blockquote when they remain literal text. An ordinary
   bracketed label is quiet.
   `caption-looking-paragraph` is opt-in: next to a table or image, a paragraph
   beginning with a caption label such as `Table 1:` or `*Figure:` may be a
   missed caption. Ordinary following prose is quiet. The default mode emits
   no `caption-looking-paragraph` finding.

   ============================================================================ *)
